WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.311 --> 00:00:03.261
- [Narrator] Whether it's to map the stars,

00:00:03.261 --> 00:00:06.009
spy on other planets, or study the sun,

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:09.377
humans have been looking
to the skies for millennia,

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:13.166
but some objects are safer
to observe than others.

00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:16.016
For example, astronomer Mark
Thompson put a pig's eye

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.121
behind a regular telescope
and aimed it at the sun,

00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:21.248
and it burned a hole
straight through the lenses

00:00:21.248 --> 00:00:23.749
in about 20 seconds.

00:00:23.749 --> 00:00:25.158
Now looking at the sun through a telescope

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.684
is an especially terrible idea,

00:00:27.684 --> 00:00:32.267
but just how bad is it to
glance up with the naked eye?

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:37.394
On a clear day the sun shines
up to 5000 times brighter

00:00:37.394 --> 00:00:39.322
than an average light bulb.

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:41.174
When something that
bright strikes your eye,

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:42.958
a few things can happen.

00:00:42.958 --> 00:00:45.695
If it's only for a moment
the worst you'll experience

00:00:45.695 --> 00:00:49.848
is a blurry splotch on your
vision called an after image.

00:00:49.848 --> 00:00:52.936
Normally light reaches the
retina at the back of your eye

00:00:52.936 --> 00:00:54.801
where it triggers photo receptors

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:57.632
that relay the information to your brain.

00:00:57.632 --> 00:01:00.606
This is how you're able to see anything,

00:01:00.606 --> 00:01:02.734
but bombard them with
too much light at once,

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:05.303
and you can actually damage
the cells and proteins

00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:08.017
that help them process light.

00:01:08.017 --> 00:01:10.480
Since your retina has no pain receptors

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.874
the damage won't hurt,

00:01:11.874 --> 00:01:14.766
but it will leave the blurry
splotch on your vision.

00:01:14.766 --> 00:01:17.043
Usually it clears up in a few minutes

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:19.710
that is unless you keep staring.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.296
Now you're doing more damage

00:01:22.296 --> 00:01:24.991
than just overloading your retina.

00:01:24.991 --> 00:01:27.283
For starters you're giving your eyes

00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:30.776
an abnormally high dose of UV radiation,

00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:33.874
the same stuff that causes sunburns.

00:01:33.874 --> 00:01:36.349
Like your skin the cornea
at the front of your eye

00:01:36.349 --> 00:01:40.323
can also burn and that will hurt.

00:01:40.323 --> 00:01:42.194
The cornea protects the rest of your eye,

00:01:42.194 --> 00:01:44.148
and is therefore covered in pain receptors

00:01:44.148 --> 00:01:47.859
that alert you whenever a
pesky eyelash is on the loose,

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:51.084
but UV radiation isn't the only issue.

00:01:51.084 --> 00:01:53.454
Too much visible light
can penetrate your eye

00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:55.787
and damage the retinal tissue

00:01:55.787 --> 00:01:59.936
which causes a condition
called solar retinitis.

00:01:59.936 --> 00:02:01.617
This means parts of retina

00:02:01.617 --> 00:02:04.178
can no longer process light normally,

00:02:04.178 --> 00:02:05.941
so you can end up with entire chunks

00:02:05.941 --> 00:02:07.684
of your vision blurred out.

00:02:07.684 --> 00:02:09.718
Depending on the extent of the damage

00:02:09.718 --> 00:02:12.175
recovery can take weeks, months,

00:02:12.175 --> 00:02:15.290
and in severe cases over a year,

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:19.386
but in rare cases the damage
is so extreme it never heals

00:02:19.386 --> 00:02:23.447
leading to a rare condition
called solar retinopathy.

00:02:23.447 --> 00:02:27.333
This usually only shows up
in reckless eclipse gazers.

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:30.613
Turns out during a solar
eclipse most of the sun's light

00:02:30.613 --> 00:02:33.094
is blocked which can
actually trick your brain

00:02:33.094 --> 00:02:35.761
into thinking it's safe to stare.

00:02:35.761 --> 00:02:38.720
Normally our bodies have a
built-in defense mechanism

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.810
against staring at the sun.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:43.657
Specifically when we squint we look up

00:02:43.657 --> 00:02:46.311
which minimizes the
amount of light coming in

00:02:46.311 --> 00:02:48.887
and protects our cornea and retina,

00:02:48.887 --> 00:02:51.546
but during a solar eclipse
the sun doesn't appear

00:02:51.546 --> 00:02:54.728
bright enough to trigger
our defense mechanisms,

00:02:54.728 --> 00:02:58.344
so we can end up staring
for longer than is safe.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.410
Suffice to say there's nothing good

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.409
about staring at the sun,

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:05.741
so do your eyes a favor
and avoid looking at it.

00:03:05.741 --> 00:03:08.090
After all, there are around 6000 stars

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.573
in the night sky which
you can safely observe

00:03:10.573 --> 00:03:12.323
for as long you wish.

